1
00:00:02,214 --> 00:00:04,991
So now we know what graphs are, why we need

2
00:00:04,991 --> 00:00:08,056
them. The question now is how we can write

3
00:00:08,056 --> 00:00:09,806
code to represent a graph.

4
00:00:10,608 --> 00:00:12,839
And the important thing here is that we'll

5
00:00:12,871 --> 00:00:15,535
not be building a tree. We're not going to

6
00:00:15,664 --> 00:00:18,071
write a tree as we learned it before. We're

7
00:00:18,071 --> 00:00:20,736
not going to use that code because we have

8
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,352
no nested children. This concept of having

9
00:00:23,448 --> 00:00:26,403
children does not exist in a graph. So using

10
00:00:26,435 --> 00:00:28,254
a tree would be the wrong form of

11
00:00:28,287 --> 00:00:29,414
representing a graph.

12
00:00:30,847 --> 00:00:33,117
Our tree code is great whenever we need a

13
00:00:33,133 --> 00:00:35,340
tree, and as you learned, there are many

14
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,710
cases where you need a tree, but if you need

15
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:39,823
a graph, you can't use that tree code.

16
00:00:41,629 --> 00:00:44,047
Instead, we have 2 common approaches for

17
00:00:44,047 --> 00:00:45,848
representing a graph in code.

18
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:49,758
We can build a so-called adjacency matrix or

19
00:00:50,131 --> 00:00:53,379
an adjacency list. And with both approaches,

20
00:00:53,412 --> 00:00:56,583
the general idea is that we want to

21
00:00:57,149 --> 00:01:01,240
store and represent the different nodes and

22
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,138
the connections between nodes because that's

23
00:01:04,138 --> 00:01:06,698
what makes up a graph in the end, nodes and

24
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:07,906
the edges between nodes.

25
00:01:09,535 --> 00:01:11,918
Now let's see how an adjacency matrix would

26
00:01:11,950 --> 00:01:12,191
work.

27
00:01:12,916 --> 00:01:15,411
Let's say this is the graph which we wanna

28
00:01:15,476 --> 00:01:16,426
represent in code.

29
00:01:17,021 --> 00:01:20,094
We have 3 nodes with the values 1, 2, and 3.

30
00:01:20,437 --> 00:01:23,370
Or with the identifiers 1, 2, and 3. You can

31
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:25,525
of course store whichever kind of data you

32
00:01:25,638 --> 00:01:27,226
want in graph nodes.

33
00:01:27,796 --> 00:01:29,343
And as you can see here, we got a couple of

34
00:01:29,359 --> 00:01:31,777
connections. Please note that this is a

35
00:01:31,986 --> 00:01:34,710
directed graph with unidirectional edges

36
00:01:34,807 --> 00:01:34,968
here.

37
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:38,834
But you could use this matrix also if you

38
00:01:38,850 --> 00:01:41,397
had an undirected graph. This does not make

39
00:01:41,397 --> 00:01:41,916
a difference.

40
00:01:42,597 --> 00:01:44,122
But how would the matrix look like?

41
00:01:46,098 --> 00:01:48,650
Well, we would build a matrix with our graph

42
00:01:48,732 --> 00:01:50,238
identifiers or values.

43
00:01:50,918 --> 00:01:53,954
So we have 1, 2, 3 on both axes here.

44
00:01:54,677 --> 00:01:57,054
And then we would look for connections

45
00:01:57,086 --> 00:02:00,009
between nodes like this connection between 1

46
00:02:00,057 --> 00:02:00,523
and 2.

47
00:02:01,101 --> 00:02:03,414
And if we find such a connection, we would

48
00:02:03,414 --> 00:02:04,362
store 1

49
00:02:05,021 --> 00:02:05,759
or true

50
00:02:06,546 --> 00:02:10,305
in the intersection of those 2 identifiers

51
00:02:10,562 --> 00:02:11,574
in our matrix.

52
00:02:12,361 --> 00:02:14,642
So for example, here we would represent that

53
00:02:14,754 --> 00:02:16,216
1 points at 2,

54
00:02:16,746 --> 00:02:20,583
by storing true or 1 here in this place in

55
00:02:20,679 --> 00:02:21,498
our matrix.

56
00:02:22,364 --> 00:02:25,190
Now important, by the way, we would store 0

57
00:02:25,848 --> 00:02:29,203
for the intersection of 2 and 1 because it's

58
00:02:29,348 --> 00:02:32,446
unidirectional here. 2 does not point at 1

59
00:02:32,815 --> 00:02:35,480
and therefore we store 0 here. If it would

60
00:02:35,544 --> 00:02:38,578
be bidirectional, so if 2 would also point

61
00:02:38,594 --> 00:02:41,130
back at 1, we would store a 1 here, of

62
00:02:41,147 --> 00:02:43,201
course, but that's not the case, so it's a

63
00:02:43,233 --> 00:02:43,635
0.

64
00:02:45,346 --> 00:02:48,051
We got no connection between 1 and 1. In

65
00:02:48,083 --> 00:02:50,948
general, a node doesn't connect to itself,

66
00:02:51,238 --> 00:02:53,202
so there we always have zeros.

67
00:02:54,184 --> 00:02:56,374
And we can then move on to the next

68
00:02:56,374 --> 00:02:58,483
connection we find, 1 to 3.

69
00:02:59,996 --> 00:03:02,733
We do have this connection. There is an edge

70
00:03:02,733 --> 00:03:06,843
between 1 and 3. 1 points at 3, so we would

71
00:03:06,843 --> 00:03:08,664
store 1 or true

72
00:03:09,244 --> 00:03:10,759
in this intersection as well.

73
00:03:11,904 --> 00:03:14,006
Well, and now we can continue filling out

74
00:03:14,070 --> 00:03:14,873
this matrix.

75
00:03:15,515 --> 00:03:19,238
2 to 2 has no connection because again,

76
00:03:19,527 --> 00:03:21,822
nodes don't connect to themselves.

77
00:03:22,576 --> 00:03:24,999
2 to 3 has no connection.

78
00:03:25,529 --> 00:03:28,610
3 points at 2, but 2 doesn't point at 3.

79
00:03:29,156 --> 00:03:32,574
Therefore, we would have 0 or false here as

80
00:03:32,686 --> 00:03:32,798
well.

81
00:03:33,793 --> 00:03:34,740
3 and 1,

82
00:03:35,318 --> 00:03:37,966
we have no connection because 1 points at 3,

83
00:03:37,982 --> 00:03:40,822
but 3 doesn't point at 1. So it's a 0 or

84
00:03:40,951 --> 00:03:41,754
false. False here.

85
00:03:42,717 --> 00:03:45,189
But now we have a connection between 3 and

86
00:03:45,350 --> 00:03:46,987
2. And therefore here

87
00:03:47,678 --> 00:03:49,026
we would also store a 1

88
00:03:49,684 --> 00:03:51,916
or true in our adjacency matrix.

89
00:03:52,590 --> 00:03:54,421
And then between 3 and 3 we have no

90
00:03:54,421 --> 00:03:57,648
connection, so that's 0. This is how we

91
00:03:57,712 --> 00:04:00,794
could represent a graph in an adjacency

92
00:04:00,794 --> 00:04:01,436
matrix.

93
00:04:02,046 --> 00:04:04,069
So in the end we just store all the nodes

94
00:04:04,455 --> 00:04:06,606
and then information about the connections

95
00:04:06,718 --> 00:04:08,931
between nodes. And that's really the idea

96
00:04:08,947 --> 00:04:09,124
here.

97
00:04:10,101 --> 00:04:13,146
So now let's represent this matrix we just

98
00:04:13,146 --> 00:04:14,059
saw on the slide.

99
00:04:14,828 --> 00:04:18,626
So this matrix in JavaScript. And therefore

100
00:04:18,722 --> 00:04:20,629
for the moment, I'll stick to those dummy

101
00:04:20,661 --> 00:04:22,343
values of 1, 2, and 3.

102
00:04:23,144 --> 00:04:25,115
But of course, I wanna highlight that you

103
00:04:25,131 --> 00:04:27,182
can use different values as well.

104
00:04:28,047 --> 00:04:29,522
So how could we represent this in

105
00:04:29,618 --> 00:04:30,195
JavaScript?

106
00:04:30,820 --> 00:04:33,319
Well, a matrix in JavaScript is just an

107
00:04:33,319 --> 00:04:36,330
array full of arrays. So a 2-dimensional

108
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:36,702
array.

109
00:04:37,836 --> 00:04:39,697
So therefore we can create a new array here,

110
00:04:40,264 --> 00:04:42,465
and this array now would have 3

111
00:04:43,096 --> 00:04:46,332
nested arrays. 3 because we have 3 nodes.

112
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:49,211
You have as many nested arrays as you have

113
00:04:49,243 --> 00:04:49,615
nodes.

114
00:04:50,278 --> 00:04:52,630
So here we have one nested array, a second

115
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:55,078
nested array, and a third nested array.

116
00:04:58,026 --> 00:05:00,518
That would be our adjacency matrix.

117
00:05:02,101 --> 00:05:04,331
So that's the array we're building. Now the

118
00:05:04,331 --> 00:05:07,844
first nested array stands for the first node

119
00:05:07,844 --> 00:05:10,572
value we have, so 1, let's say. The second

120
00:05:10,572 --> 00:05:13,074
one stands for 2, the third one represents

121
00:05:13,074 --> 00:05:13,427
3.

122
00:05:14,101 --> 00:05:16,829
And now inside of those nested arrays, we

123
00:05:16,989 --> 00:05:20,984
add again 3 values to show the connection

124
00:05:21,016 --> 00:05:23,695
between the value represented by this array,

125
00:05:24,225 --> 00:05:24,417
1,

126
00:05:25,251 --> 00:05:26,455
and the other nodes.

127
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:30,270
So the first element we add to this first

128
00:05:30,270 --> 00:05:31,172
nested array

129
00:05:31,928 --> 00:05:35,487
is a representation of the connection from

130
00:05:35,487 --> 00:05:38,842
the node with value 1 to the node with value

131
00:05:38,939 --> 00:05:39,068
1.

132
00:05:39,746 --> 00:05:42,070
Now, as I mentioned, nodes don't connect to

133
00:05:42,183 --> 00:05:44,459
themselves, so you would enter false

134
00:05:45,234 --> 00:05:46,436
or 0 here.

135
00:05:48,633 --> 00:05:51,157
The second element in this first nested

136
00:05:51,157 --> 00:05:54,188
array represents the connection from the

137
00:05:54,349 --> 00:05:57,947
first node with value 1 to the second node

138
00:05:58,205 --> 00:05:59,060
with value 2.

139
00:06:00,884 --> 00:06:03,030
And if you recall, we did have such a

140
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:05,044
connection here, so we should insert

141
00:06:06,994 --> 00:06:07,590
a 1 here.

142
00:06:08,605 --> 00:06:11,442
The third value in that first nested array

143
00:06:11,748 --> 00:06:14,879
represents the connection from value 1, so

144
00:06:14,911 --> 00:06:18,849
from node 1 to node 3 with value 3. We did

145
00:06:18,849 --> 00:06:21,190
have that connection as well, so this is a 1

146
00:06:21,636 --> 00:06:21,941
as well.

147
00:06:23,241 --> 00:06:24,187
And we continue like this.

148
00:06:24,781 --> 00:06:27,686
The second nested array represents node 2

149
00:06:27,894 --> 00:06:30,526
with a value of 2, and the first value is

150
00:06:30,542 --> 00:06:32,676
the connection from node 2 to node 1.

151
00:06:33,350 --> 00:06:36,464
Node 1 points at 2, but 2 doesn't point at

152
00:06:36,496 --> 00:06:37,683
1, so that's a 0.

153
00:06:38,502 --> 00:06:41,871
2 to 2 has no connection because nodes don't

154
00:06:41,871 --> 00:06:44,359
connect to themselves, so that's a 0.

155
00:06:45,225 --> 00:06:47,809
And node 2 also has no connection to 3 in

156
00:06:47,809 --> 00:06:49,975
this example, So that's also a 0.

157
00:06:51,339 --> 00:06:52,895
And now for the last node here,

158
00:06:53,825 --> 00:06:54,692
that's node 3.

159
00:06:55,253 --> 00:06:57,146
It has no connection to node 1.

160
00:06:57,740 --> 00:07:00,884
I mean, node 1 points at node 3, but node 3

161
00:07:00,948 --> 00:07:03,740
doesn't point at node 1 in this example, so

162
00:07:03,756 --> 00:07:04,478
that's a 0.

163
00:07:05,520 --> 00:07:07,638
It does have a connection to 2 though.

164
00:07:08,889 --> 00:07:11,216
You can see that in our example, node 3

165
00:07:11,472 --> 00:07:12,980
points at node 2,

166
00:07:13,959 --> 00:07:14,713
and therefore,

167
00:07:16,030 --> 00:07:17,058
Here we insert a 1.

168
00:07:17,733 --> 00:07:19,485
And last but not least, it does not have a

169
00:07:19,485 --> 00:07:21,815
connection with itself, so that's a 0.

170
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:25,864
And that would be our adjacency matrix for

171
00:07:25,912 --> 00:07:27,471
this simple example.

172
00:07:28,001 --> 00:07:30,396
Now if we now, for example, want to find out

173
00:07:30,572 --> 00:07:32,549
if there is a connection between, let's say,

174
00:07:32,677 --> 00:07:34,284
node 2 and node 3,

175
00:07:34,830 --> 00:07:37,434
we would simply access our matrix for the

176
00:07:37,578 --> 00:07:39,137
index of node 2,

177
00:07:39,844 --> 00:07:40,841
so that's 1, 1,

178
00:07:41,501 --> 00:07:44,431
indexes start at 0, so node 2 has an index

179
00:07:44,431 --> 00:07:44,753
of 1.

180
00:07:45,542 --> 00:07:47,861
And then on that, we would access the index

181
00:07:47,861 --> 00:07:50,830
of node 3, which is 2, again because indexes

182
00:07:50,960 --> 00:07:51,675
start at 0.

183
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:55,052
And we would get 0 as a result and we would

184
00:07:55,084 --> 00:07:57,390
know that there is no connection between

185
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:58,895
node 2 and node 3.

186
00:08:01,392 --> 00:08:04,339
If we want to know all connections node 1

187
00:08:04,516 --> 00:08:04,790
has,

188
00:08:05,321 --> 00:08:07,713
we would simply insert the index of node 1,

189
00:08:07,913 --> 00:08:09,799
which is 0, it's this array,

190
00:08:10,628 --> 00:08:12,217
And we would get back our list of

191
00:08:12,249 --> 00:08:14,208
connections of node 1.

192
00:08:14,818 --> 00:08:16,906
And now we would just need to filter that

193
00:08:16,906 --> 00:08:19,234
list. So in the end here, we would have to

194
00:08:19,234 --> 00:08:23,649
filter and check that the edge is greater

195
00:08:23,649 --> 00:08:24,211
than 0

196
00:08:25,239 --> 00:08:27,310
and we would get a list of all existing

197
00:08:27,342 --> 00:08:29,430
connections. Though of course here we can't

198
00:08:29,430 --> 00:08:31,325
see to which other nodes this is connected,

199
00:08:31,662 --> 00:08:33,492
but we could see the total number of

200
00:08:33,508 --> 00:08:36,302
connections this node has. To see to which

201
00:08:36,431 --> 00:08:38,825
nodes it connects, we could use this array.

202
00:08:39,532 --> 00:08:41,444
So that's how we can work with an adjacency

203
00:08:41,476 --> 00:08:42,118
matrix.

204
00:08:42,761 --> 00:08:45,958
Now if you wonder where the actual values of

205
00:08:45,958 --> 00:08:48,127
the nodes are stored, well, there are

206
00:08:48,127 --> 00:08:48,882
different approaches.

207
00:08:49,670 --> 00:08:51,035
You could simply have a separate array,

208
00:08:51,662 --> 00:08:54,747
nodes, where you just have 3 values because

209
00:08:54,747 --> 00:08:58,008
we have 3 nodes and then every node value

210
00:08:58,008 --> 00:09:00,113
could just be a number or if it's more

211
00:09:00,193 --> 00:09:02,523
complex, you could have an object, maybe

212
00:09:02,587 --> 00:09:04,658
with an ID and then the actual value.

213
00:09:05,253 --> 00:09:08,272
So that's really up to you. The node values

214
00:09:08,384 --> 00:09:12,624
can be stored separated from the adjacency

215
00:09:12,624 --> 00:09:13,218
matrix

216
00:09:14,246 --> 00:09:16,623
because the adjacency matrix is really just

217
00:09:16,639 --> 00:09:18,598
used to represent the connections,

218
00:09:19,208 --> 00:09:21,858
which is the key of a graph. And often when

219
00:09:21,858 --> 00:09:24,090
you work with a graph, it's all about those

220
00:09:24,187 --> 00:09:24,684
connections.

221
00:09:25,230 --> 00:09:28,185
Getting the actual node values will rarely

222
00:09:28,282 --> 00:09:30,930
be a challenge because again, you can't just

223
00:09:30,930 --> 00:09:33,322
store those in a simple array totally

224
00:09:33,434 --> 00:09:36,002
detached from the connections. You just need

225
00:09:36,002 --> 00:09:38,505
to make sure that your identifier here

226
00:09:39,051 --> 00:09:41,731
allows you to retrieve the connections for

227
00:09:41,779 --> 00:09:43,287
that node in the matrix.

228
00:09:44,026 --> 00:09:46,240
But that is how you could work with an

229
00:09:46,272 --> 00:09:49,049
adjacency matrix. I did mention that this is

230
00:09:49,081 --> 00:09:51,648
just one way of working with graphs though.

231
00:09:52,050 --> 00:09:54,216
The alternative is the adjacency list.
